# Files that are exempt from the test-coverage check.
# One path per line, relative to the binate repo root.
# Example: pkg/builtins/rt/rt.bn

# pkg/bootstrap/bootstrap.bn: format helpers for the print/println
# builtin (formatInt, formatBool). The test-runner infrastructure
# can't currently run unit tests in pkg/bootstrap as a regular
# Binate package — the synthetic test runner has shape issues with
# the @[]char return type (`return ""` reads back as len > 0).
# Likely needs a forked test runner for bootstrap, in line with
# the future conformance-test fork the user has in mind. The
# helpers themselves are exercised end-to-end by the conformance
# suite's println tests across all modes.
pkg/bootstrap/bootstrap.bn

# === GENUINE EXCEPTIONS — internal helpers / per-platform ABI ========
# Files that legitimately have no per-file _test.bn, exercised instead by a
# package-level test (and conformance / e2e where noted).  The standard is a
# sibling _test.bn per non-test .bn; this list should hold only genuine
# exceptions (no "TEMPORARY, add a test later" entries — those get a test).
#
# Math internal helpers + constants + an operation-split type:
#   bessel01_asymp / trig_reduce are unexported helpers (no public surface to
#   test directly — covered through the public bessel/trig functions); const is
#   just constants; big/nat is a type exercised across nat_arith/div/shift_test.
impls/stdlib/pkg/std/math/bessel01_asymp.bn
impls/stdlib/pkg/std/math/big/nat.bn
impls/stdlib/pkg/std/math/const.bn
impls/stdlib/pkg/std/math/trig_reduce.bn
#
# os per-platform ABI files — each is platform-specific by construction, NOT
# sprawl to amalgamate (decided 2026-06-20):
#   * stat_{darwin,linux_aarch64,linux_arm32,linux_x64}: each defines a DISTINCT
#     kernel `struct stat` layout for its platform; only the active one compiles
#     (its #[build] gate).  Tested via stat_test.bn (the active platform) +
#     e2e/stat-values.sh against real libc stat values.
#   * os_errno{,_darwin,_linux}: the errno accessor is split per-OS because
#     __c_call needs a LITERAL symbol that must link — one body referencing both
#     __error (darwin) and __errno_location (linux) would fail to link the
#     wrong-OS symbol.  Exercised through every os call that reports an errno.
#   * os_baremetal: the freestanding os stub (#[build] baremetal); bare metal
#     runs no host unit tests (shares the bootstrap exception above).
#   * readdir_{darwin,linux64,linux_arm32}: each defines a DISTINCT `struct
#     dirent` layout (the d_type/d_name offsets) for its platform; only the
#     active one compiles (its #[build] gate).  readdir_linux64 covers both
#     64-bit Linux arches (x64 + aarch64 share the dirent layout, unlike struct
#     stat).  direntType/direntName are exercised via the active platform's
#     ReadDir (conformance/stdlib/os/006_readdir); the shared d_type->FileMode
#     mapping is unit-tested in readdir_test.bn.
impls/stdlib/pkg/std/os/os_baremetal.bn
impls/stdlib/pkg/std/os/os_errno.bn
impls/stdlib/pkg/std/os/os_errno_darwin.bn
impls/stdlib/pkg/std/os/os_errno_linux.bn
impls/stdlib/pkg/std/os/readdir_darwin.bn
impls/stdlib/pkg/std/os/readdir_linux64.bn
impls/stdlib/pkg/std/os/readdir_linux_arm32.bn
impls/stdlib/pkg/std/os/stat_darwin.bn
impls/stdlib/pkg/std/os/stat_linux_aarch64.bn
impls/stdlib/pkg/std/os/stat_linux_arm32.bn
impls/stdlib/pkg/std/os/stat_linux_x64.bn
#
# Bare-metal bootstrap/rt variants — share the bootstrap exception above (the
# synthetic test runner can't run them as packages).
impls/core/baremetal/pkg/bootstrap/bootstrap.bn
impls/core/common/pkg/builtins/rt/rt_baremetal.bn
